<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_COARSE_LOCATION" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION" />
    <!-- For the mushroom log's camera capture (ActivityResultContracts.TakePicture) — see
         com.forager.app.photo.CameraCaptureFiles. Gallery attach needs no permission at all: it
         uses PickVisualMedia, the modern photo picker. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.CAMERA" />
    <!-- Photo-geodata dispatch: lets a gallery import's EXIF read see the location tag a photo's
         own MediaStore entry carries (MediaStore.setRequireOriginal, API 29+) — without it, the
         platform silently redacts GPS EXIF from any stream this app opens. Requested at the moment
         of first import, not app launch — see com.forager.app.ui.log.PhotoAcquisitionLaunchers. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_MEDIA_LOCATION" />

    <!-- For com.forager.app.service.TrackRecordingService, a foreground service so a multi-hour
         track recording keeps running (and stays visibly running to the user) while the app is
         backgrounded. FOREGROUND_SERVICE_LOCATION is required as of API 34 alongside the general
         FOREGROUND_SERVICE permission for a service declared with foregroundServiceType="location"
         below. POST_NOTIFICATIONS is a runtime permission as of API 33 — this only declares it;
         requesting it is UI work for Phase 1c, which is what actually starts this service. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE_LOCATION" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS" />
    <!-- Field-test dispatch item 4: DetectOffTrackUseCase's off-track signal reaching a pocketed
         phone needs a real vibration, not just a notification post — LOW-importance channels
         (see TrackRecordingService's own "track_recording" channel, reused for this alert) don't
         vibrate through the notification system on their own. VIBRATE is a normal permission —
         granted at install, no runtime prompt needed. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.VIBRATE" />

    <!--
      Without this, PlannedTripRow's "Directions" action always reports no maps app installed,
      even with one present: compileSdk/targetSdk 37 means Android's package-visibility rules
      apply, so `PackageManager.resolveActivity()`/`startActivity()` for an implicit `geo:` intent
      report nothing resolvable unless the manifest explicitly declares visibility into it — this
      is Android's own documented pattern for exactly that intent (see the platform docs on
      package visibility and the `geo:` scheme example).
    -->
    <queries>
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW" />
            <data android:scheme="geo" />
        </intent>
        <!--
          Same package-visibility rule as the geo: query above, for
          launchINaturalistObservation's own explicit Intent.setPackage(INATURALIST_PACKAGE) call
          (AvailabilityScreen.kt). Without this, PackageManager.resolveActivity() for that
          explicitly-package-targeted intent reports no match on API 30+ even with the iNaturalist
          app installed — its own resolveActivity() check would then always fall through to the
          plain implicit web intent, silently never reaching the installed app. A <package> element,
          not another <intent> filter: Intent.setPackage narrows resolution to one already-known
          package name, which is exactly what <queries><package> declares visibility into, per
          Android's own package-visibility docs.
        -->
        <package android:name="org.inaturalist.android" />
    </queries>

    <!--
      android:allowBackup="false" is a BETA-SCOPED decision (finding B4, 2026-09-09) and MUST be
      revisited before a production release — see
      docs/audits/2026-09-09-allow-backup-and-play-signing-notes.md.

      Why off for the beta: with allowBackup="true" and neither android:dataExtractionRules nor
      android:fullBackupContent declared (res/xml/ holds only file_paths.xml), Android Auto Backup
      and device-to-device transfer copy everything under the app's data directory by default —
      including the live Room database and the photo files. A Room database is copied while it may
      be mid-write, and restoring a torn SQLite snapshot (twelve migrations of history; app/schemas/
      holds 4.json–15.json) onto a fresh install is a known corruption path. The field journal that
      would corrupt is exactly the data this closed beta exists to collect, so the downside of a bad
      restore is worse than the downside of no restore.

      Alternative rejected: keep allowBackup="true" and write android:dataExtractionRules (API 31+)
      plus android:fullBackupContent (API 30 and below) that exclude the database directory and the
      photo directory. That is the right long-term answer — it keeps settings backed up while
      leaving the fragile data out — but it is two rule files that have to be correct across two
      backup APIs and two transports (cloud backup and D2D transfer), verified against real restore
      behaviour that nothing in this repo's Robolectric suite can exercise. That is more code to get
      right than a 14-day closed test needs, and testers are not relying on restore during it.

      Consequence, accepted: a tester who changes device or reinstalls loses their journal
      outright, and this also turns off the device-to-device transfer that
      docs/plans/journal-trips-and-offline-regions.md (the "Leak paths" list) intended to keep —
      that plan's line "Excluding both would silently lose years of field notes on a phone upgrade"
      is the cost being taken on here, knowingly and only for the beta. GPX export covers tracks and
      waypoints, not the journal, so it is not a substitute.
    -->
    <application
        android:name=".ForagerApplication"
        android:allowBackup="false"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:supportsRtl="true"
        android:theme="@style/Theme.Forager">

        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:theme="@style/Theme.Forager">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>

        <service
            android:name=".service.TrackRecordingService"
            android:exported="false"
            android:foregroundServiceType="location" />

        <!-- Grants the camera app write access to a file under filesDir/captures/ for
             ActivityResultContracts.TakePicture, without exposing broader file access — see
             com.forager.app.photo.CameraCaptureFiles and res/xml/file_paths.xml. -->
        <provider
            android:name="androidx.core.content.FileProvider"
            android:authorities="${applicationId}.fileprovider"
            android:exported="false"
            android:grantUriPermissions="true">
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.support.FILE_PROVIDER_PATHS"
                android:resource="@xml/file_paths" />
        </provider>
    </application>

</manifest>
